# Tether language and product spec

This file is the repo host (`root.tether`). It describes tether itself.

@ref src/extract/types.ts#Tether
@public

doc {

# Invariants

- Git is required. Extraction and facts are functions of the tree plus history. There is no second ledger.
- Location is the bind. A comment on a declaration binds to that declaration. A sidecar binds to the sibling file or directory. `root.tether` binds to the repository.
- No bind table. Optional `@ref` and `@symbol` are extra named targets, never the host.
- Facts only. The tool emits only what git + extract + AST can recompute. No severity, no age, no "meaningful change," no attest command.
- No independent doctrine files. Tracked `*.md` and `*.txt` are `rogue_document` unless allowlisted or honorary.
- The full wiki is derived and never committed. Search indexes the extract, not the wiki.
- `README.md` may mix authored prose with one generated region. That region is a view of `@public` tethers, linted against compile. It is not a second source of doctrine.
- Rubber-stamp exists: touching tether bytes refreshes provenance. Irreducible without reading the prose. Not a judgment layer.

# Hosts

| artifact | host |
|---|---|
| marked comment immediately preceding a declaration | that declaration (symbol) |
| `foo.ts.tether` beside `foo.ts` | file `foo.ts` |
| `src.tether` beside directory `src/` | folder `src/` |
| `root.tether` at repo root | repository |
| `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` | honorary folder of their directory |

Do not use a `.tether` file inside the folder as a second folder convention.

# Language

Closed forms. The file host is not markdown. Prose lives in `doc` bodies and may use markdown *inside* those bodies.

```
@symbol Name
@symbol Name.member
@ref path#Name
@ref #Name
@ref path
@public

doc {
  doctrine
}

example ts {
  await refreshSession(cookie)
}
```

- `@symbol Name` is a claim on the host file (inline comment or `foo.ts.tether`). The file is obvious. Gone → `symbol_missing`. Two of that name in the file → `symbol_ambiguous`. Bare `@symbol` on a folder or root tether is `ill_formed`.
- Paths are scoped to the host. You can only name what that host can see. `../` is `ill_formed`.
  - inline / `foo.ts.tether`: `@symbol Name` or `@ref #Name` (this file). `@ref sibling.ts#Other` is a child of the same directory.
  - `src.tether`: `@ref extract/types.ts#Tether` (child of `src/`).
  - `root.tether`: `@ref src/extract/types.ts#Tether` (child of the repo).
- Distant claims belong on the folder or root that can see them. Public contracts at the root use the full repo path.
- `@public` includes this tether in the public compile (folder-mirrored headings/nav, and the generated README region). Omit it: private wiki only.
- Multiple `@symbol` / `@ref` lines are a union. `@public` is a flag, not a target.
- `doc { ... }` is opaque prose. Unfenced trailing text after directives is also body.
- `example <lang> { ... }` is an opaque snippet. It is not a host, not a `@ref`, not a declaration, not a Quartz node. Extract does not parse it as code. Compile renders it as a fenced block. Use this for illustrations; do not `@ref` names that exist only inside an example.
- Fenced code inside `doc` is equally opaque. Prefer `example` so the intent is structural.
- No `@quartz` until Quartz is a released dependency.
- Inline form: a comment whose first token after the marker is `@tether`, then the same directives, `doc`, and `example` bodies.

```
/** @tether
 * @ref session.ts#Session
 * Refresh is a rename of session state, not an in-place patch.
 */
export const refreshSession = ...
```

Line comments: consecutive `// @tether` then `//` continuation lines, immediately above the declaration.

# Extract

- Adjacency: marked comment immediately preceding the declaration. Only whitespace (and language skip nodes: decorators, attributes) may sit between them. Not nearest-enclosing.
- Sidecars never auto-bind to symbols.
- Folder fingerprint = sorted tracked path + blob hash under that folder. Noisy on purpose.
- `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` are not doctrine. They are not rogue and they are not extracted. `SKILL.md` is the same: out of scope. We do not parse, compile, or babysit their bodies.
- Fingerprint = grammar-version-prefixed hash of preorder AST node types + token text, no positions, no comment extras. Rename of an emitted identifier changes it. Reformat does not.
- Shape fingerprint = fingerprint with the declaration name field replaced by `$NAME`. Used only to *list* rename candidates, never to auto-rebind.

# Facts (closed)

| kind | proven when |
|---|---|
| `rogue_document` | tracked `*.md`/`*.txt` not on the allowlist and not honorary |
| `ill_formed` | parse error, `@symbol` disagrees with adjacency, `@symbol` on a folder/root tether, or a `../` path |
| `duplicate_id` | two tethers share an explicit id |
| `host_missing` | derived host path, symbol, or directory is gone |
| `host_fingerprint_changed` | host fingerprint at HEAD ≠ fingerprint at last commit that touched the tether |
| `ref_missing` | optional `@ref` target not found |
| `ref_fingerprint_changed` | that `@ref` target's fingerprint changed since the tether last changed |
| `symbol_missing` | `@symbol` on a file host names a declaration that is not in that file |
| `symbol_ambiguous` | `@symbol` matches more than one declaration in that file |
| `public_surface_stale` | the generated span in `README.md` (or the public tree hash) ≠ current `@public` compile |

`ref_missing` may include `candidates` (N≤4) only when each candidate is a unique shape match in the same file or across the two git blobs. Otherwise omit the list.

`example` bodies never contribute symbols, refs, fingerprints of foreign code, or Quartz graph nodes. Changing an example only touches the tether (provenance), not a host fingerprint.

No rename kind. A rename is `host_fingerprint_changed` (comment moved with the decl) or `host_missing` (it did not).

# Allowlist

Default allowlist is those names **at the repo root only** (`README.md`, not `docs/README.md`). Honorary / out of scope (not rogue, not extracted): `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `SKILL.md`. `.tether.json` may add extra full paths.

# CLI

JSON-first, Effect, Bun, agentic-cli family. No project-local `.tether/` cache. State under `$TETHER_HOME` or `~/.config/tether/projects/<git-key>/`. Git key = normalized origin URL, else hash of repo root.

Commands: `doctor`, `capabilities`, `schema`, `examples`, `extract`, `lint`, `compile`, `search`, `index`.

`lint` prints facts. Exit 1 if any fact kind is in `fail_on` (default: all kinds above). That map is the only judgment, and it is config.

`compile` writes two derived trees under the project cache dir, never as new repo files:

- `wiki/` — every tether, fact frontmatter, stacked bodies (symbol → file sidecar → enclosing folders → root).
- `public/` — only `@public` tethers, same folder mirror, plus a generated nav.

`compile` also rewrites exactly one region in allowlisted `README.md`, between:

```
<!-- tether:public -->
<!-- /tether:public -->
```

Authored text outside those markers is never touched. Inside: generated headings/nav (and the public `doc` / `example` render) from the `@public` tree. If the markers are missing, compile does not edit README; lint emits `public_surface_stale` when any `@public` tether exists.

`search` is lexical + semantic over the extract (SQLite FTS5, local embeddings, fusion). Not session search. Example bodies are searchable as text, not as symbols.

# Merge

Same host + bind stacks. Innermost first. Duplicate explicit ids are `duplicate_id`. Bodies are not fused into one essay.

# Languages (v1 extract)

javascript, typescript, tsx, rust, go, ruby, python via `web-tree-sitter` and grammar wasm. Language tables own comment kinds, skip kinds, declaration kinds, and name extraction. Swift is out until a loadable grammar exists.

Product: every named declaration the grammar can see (functions, types, methods, fields, …) is a possible host. One parse per file. Incremental on dirty files is enough for normal repos. The limiter is not node count — it is ambiguous names in the same file (`symbol_ambiguous`). A tether never searches the whole repo for a name.

Elixir and C++ are out of this cut.

# Out of v1

Quartz-powered refs. Session search. A second attest command. Mild warnings. Bind tables. A committed `docs/` wiki. Examples participating in the symbol graph.

# Bootstrap release and stabilization handoff (2026-09-04)

Version 0.1.0 is intentionally experimental. The maintainer authorized publication
before stabilization to unblock development away from the original machine.

## Completed infrastructure

- GitHub `skastr0/tether` is public. Bootstrap source and workflows are on `main`.
- npm `@skastr0/tether` and `@skastr0/tether-{darwin,linux}-{arm64,x64}` are published
  at 0.1.0. Registry integrity matched all five locally packed tarballs.
- `.agents/setup` prepares Bun 1.3.14, Node >=22.14 and frozen dependencies without
  gating orb startup on tests. A fresh orb passed setup twice, a clean login-shell
  toolchain check, typecheck, two release-script tests, and source capabilities/doctor.
- Each package's npm Trusted Publisher was created and read back: GitHub owner
  `skastr0`, repository `tether`, workflow filename `npm-publish.yml`, environment
  `release`, direct publishing allowed. `npm trust list <package> --json` inspects it.
- GitHub's `release` environment requires `skastr0` approval and permits `v*` tags.
  No local npm credential is required for future OIDC publication from that workflow.

## Known failure, not yet fixed

`bun run test` invokes Vitest under Node. Git helpers in `src/core/git.ts`,
`src/facts/lint.ts`, and `src/extract/walk.ts` call `Bun.spawn`, which is unavailable
there. The measured local suite had 64 failures and 191 passes before the new asset
test was added. GitHub CI independently failed with `GitCommandError: Bun is not defined`:
https://github.com/skastr0/tether/actions/runs/33929502643

This is a shared runtime incompatibility, not 64 independently diagnosed defects.
Seventeen test-only Host union typing errors were already fixed; typecheck passes.
Do not hide the remaining failures with skipped tests or a fake Bun global.

## Next actions, in order

1. Make the Git subprocess implementation work in both Node tests and Bun runtime.
   A shared `node:child_process` implementation is the proposed fix, not implemented.
   Preserve raw stdout in facts/extract (git blobs are source text), trimming in core
   where it already occurs, exit codes, ENOENT handling, and typed errors. Include
   process cancellation and tests. Then run `bun run verify` until genuinely green.
2. Run `bun run pack:npm-cli` and `bun run smoke:npm-cli`. All four binaries compiled
   and tarball content/integrity checks passed, but installed-package execution has
   NOT been verified. The smoke harness itself is new and needs its first real run.
   Check all seven grammars, extraction, persistent SQLite FTS5 search, relocation,
   missing assets, and operation without Bun on PATH. Source doctor is not this proof.
3. Get `.github/workflows/ci.yml` green on all four native runner architectures.
   Its pack/smoke jobs are currently blocked by the failed verification job.
4. Release a new synchronized version, normally 0.1.1: update root package version,
   build/verify/commit/push, create the matching `v0.1.1` tag, and approve `release`.
   `scripts/build-npm-cli.ts` generates all five manifests from the root version;
   `scripts/publish-npm.mjs` publishes exact tarballs, platforms first and wrapper last.
   Observe the first actual OIDC upload and verify provenance plus registry installs.
   Trusted Publisher configuration is verified; successful OIDC publication is NOT.

Never republish 0.1.0 or bypass CI to claim stabilization. A partial future upload
requires inspecting exact registry versions/integrity before resuming; differing
published bytes require a new version. The bootstrap did not create a release tag
or GitHub Release. Windows and Linux musl remain unsupported.

}
